<section class="home-thesis hr" id="thesis">
  <div class="hc">
    <h2>Your EHR keeps the record. <em>Stream keeps the project.</em></h2>
    <p class="home-thesis-lede">Every patient is a project: problems that evolve, plans that change, follow-ups that have to happen. Good clinicians keep that project by hand, in their notes. Stream keeps it for you, alongside whatever EHR you already use.</p>
    <div class="home-thesis-grid">
      <div class="home-thesis-card home-thesis-card--muted">
        <div class="home-thesis-tag">The system you keep by hand</div>
        <h3>It works. <em>Until it doesn't.</em></h3>
        <ul class="home-thesis-list">
          <li><strong>It doesn't scale.</strong> It holds for the patients you remember, not a full panel.</li>
          <li><strong>It doesn't transfer.</strong> It lives in your shorthand, so a partner or covering clinician starts from scratch.</li>
          <li><strong>It takes upkeep.</strong> The running summary gets re-typed and carried forward, and it's the first thing to slip on a hard week.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="home-thesis-card home-thesis-card--stream">
        <div class="home-thesis-tag">Stream</div>
        <h3>The same system, <em>built as you document.</em></h3>
        <p>Every visit's Assessment &amp; Plan is filed under its problem, so each patient's project assembles itself — and any clinician in your practice can open it.</p>
        <p class="home-thesis-fine">Stream doesn't replace your EHR or integrate with it. The note pastes in, and your EHR stays the record.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

